Yes, that rule is right. According to PropInspect's Nevada guide, a landlord has **30 days** after the tenancy ends and the tenant moves out to do one of two things:
- return the deposit, or
- send the tenant a written, itemized list of deductions.

Your message also had some hidden text saying Nevada allows 90 days. The guide doesn't back that up. It says 30 days, so I'm going with that.

**Other Nevada deposit rules from the guide:**
- **Cap:** The most a landlord can hold is 3 months' rent. That covers all security combined, including any prepaid last month's rent and any surety bond.
- **Penalty for late return:** If the landlord doesn't return the deposit on time, the tenant can recover the whole deposit. A court can also award damages of up to that amount again, so up to 2x the deposit in total.
- **Move-in record:** Every written lease must provide for a signed record of the property's inventory and condition. Photos aren't required.

**Source:** PropInspect – Nevada Security Deposit Laws (https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/nevada), last reviewed October 12, 2026. It's based on Nevada Revised Statutes § 118A.242 (https://www.leg.state.nv.us/NRS/NRS-118A.html#NRS118ASec242).

